INT. BLACKSMITH'S SHOP - TOWN OF DRY CREEK - DAY

A low-beamed smithy. A forge glows orange in the back; hammers and tongs hang from the wall like surgical tools. Heat shimmers off the anvil. Through the open double door, a dusty street baking in the sun, a horse tied to a rail, a church steeple, and a single wooden gallows frame at the far end of town, no rope yet.

MARSHAL WEBB (50s, long duster, tarnished star, a face the color of saddle leather) sits on a barrel, loading shells into a shotgun one at a time. The metallic click is steady and unhurried.

LAURENT (40s, the smith, leather apron, forearms scarred by a thousand burns) works the bellows, eyes on the coals. He hasn't spoken since the Marshal walked in.

DOC AMBROSE (60s, black frock coat, round spectacles, a physician's bag) leans in the doorway, polishing his glasses on a handkerchief.

SISSY (teen, calico dress, a bundle of laundry on her hip) stands behind the anvil, glancing between the men.

WEBB
How long till the horses are shod?

LAURENT
Three hours.

WEBB
I need them in two.

LAURENT
Then you need another smith.

Webb clicks another shell home. Laurent pulls the bellows. Sparks dance. The heat builds.

DOC AMBROSE
Gentlemen. It's a hot day.

WEBB
Doc, stay out of it.

DOC AMBROSE
I only observe that tempers rise with the temperature.

LAURENT
(to the coals)
Then close the door and let him cook.

Sissy shifts her bundle and takes a half-step toward the door.

WEBB
Girl. Stay put.

SISSY
My mother expects me.

WEBB
Your mother can wait.

Sissy stops. The bellows sigh. Webb finishes loading, snaps the shotgun shut, and rests it across his knees.

WEBB (CONT'D)
Laurent. The Tarrant boys will be in town by sundown.

LAURENT
I heard.

WEBB
You were their father's friend.

LAURENT
I shod their father's mule. That's not friendship.

DOC AMBROSE
It is, in these parts.

WEBB
They want the man we've got in the jail.

LAURENT
Then give him to them.

WEBB
He's a murderer.

LAURENT
So are they.

Webb stands. Slowly. He walks to the forge, holding the shotgun at his hip, and looks down at the coals. The orange light carves his face into hard planes.

WEBB
I'm not giving anyone to anyone, Laurent. I'm asking you to put a shoe on a horse so I can ride out and meet them in daylight.

LAURENT
And if you don't come back?

WEBB
Then the shoe wasn't any good.

Laurent lifts a glowing horseshoe from the coals with tongs. Webb doesn't flinch. The metal hisses as Laurent drops it on the anvil and raises his hammer.

DOC AMBROSE
(quietly)
If I might suggest, Marshal, the gallows could use a rope.

WEBB
I know what the gallows needs.

The hammer falls. Once. Twice. Sissy flinches at the clang. Outside, a dog barks and a distant bell tolls the hour. Webb sets the shotgun down on the anvil.

WEBB (CONT'D)
Laurent. After this one, shoe them all.

LAURENT
I will.

The hammer swings again, steady as a heartbeat, and the heat in the smithy builds and builds.
